diff --git a/docs/LDN-SETTINGS.md b/docs/LDN-SETTINGS.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..225cc5f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/LDN-SETTINGS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,73 @@
+# Online play settings (LDN relay)
+
+This page designs the in-app settings for [LDN relay multiplayer](LDN-RELAY.md). Players on iPhone and Mac turn online play on, pick a relay server, set a player name and an optional room password, test the connection and share an invite with friends. Nobody has to set an environment variable.
+
+## Goals
+
+- Online play works on iPhone, where environment variables cannot be set, and on Mac, without a terminal.
+- Friends join by pasting one invite, and switch servers with one tap.
+- Off by default. With online play off nothing changes: no variable is set and the host behaves exactly as before.
+- An isolated module. The host still reads only the `DM_LDNR*` variables (LDN-RELAY.md, Configure), so the relay client and its tests are unchanged. The launchers gain one section and a few hook lines.
+
+## Mechanism
+
+Both game launchers already work the same way: the settings screen runs before the game starts and puts the player's choices into the process environment, which the host reads (iOS `dm/ios/Sources/Launcher.swift`, Mac `dm/mac/Launcher.swift`, both on the model in `LauncherSettings.swift`). Online play uses the same path.
+
+- `dm/ios/Sources/OnlinePlay.swift` holds the model, the validation, the invite format, the connection test and the environment it writes. It needs only Foundation (POSIX sockets for the test), so the same file builds for iOS, for the Mac launcher and for the tests.
+- `dm/ios/Sources/OnlinePlayView.swift` is the SwiftUI section, with small `#if os(iOS)` / `os(macOS)` branches (the device name, the clipboard).
+- Play calls `OnlinePlayModel.apply()`, which sets or clears `DM_LDNR`, `DM_LDNR_SERVER`, `DM_LDNR_NAME`, `DM_LDNR_ROOM_PASSWORD` and `DM_LDNR_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_PASSWORD`. When the screen is not shown (iOS `DM_LAUNCHER=0`, Mac "Show this window at launch" off) the saved settings apply in the same way.
+- **The environment wins.** A `DM_LDNR*` variable set by an env file (`Documents/env.txt`, the bundle's `dm/env.txt`, the Mac app's `env.txt`) or by the launch environment is never replaced. The section shows that value and marks it as set by env.txt. Scripts and front ends that use the variables keep working.
+- `DM_LDNR_LOG` stays an environment-only diagnostic switch.
+
+## Where settings live
+
+| Setting | iPhone | Mac |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Online play on or off | this game's app | this game's app |
+| Saved servers, chosen server, player name, room password, plaintext consent | this game's app | shared by every game app (`com.nx2apple.online` preferences) |
+
+On iPhone every game is its own app and cannot read another app's settings without an App Group entitlement, which a sideloaded build may not have. The invite closes that gap: paste the same invite into each game. On Mac the game apps are not sandboxed, so the connection details are set once for all games, and each game only decides whether it plays online.
+
+The room password is stored in the app's preferences (UserDefaults), not the Keychain. A room password is meant to be shared in an invite, and Copy invite has to read it back, so it is not kept as a secret of the device.
+
+Keys: `online.enabled` (Bool, per game), `online.servers` (JSON array of `{"name", "address"}`), `online.server` (the chosen address), `online.name`, `online.password`, `online.plaintextConsent` (Bool).
+
+## The Online play section
+
+The section sits on the settings screen below the game's own settings. On Mac it is part of the basic settings, above Cloud saves. Every string has English and Simplified Chinese text, picked from the system language.
+
+1. **Online play** toggle (联机游戏). Footer: "Local wireless play reaches friends over the internet through a relay server. Everyone picks the same server, and the same room password when one is set." When off, the rest of the section is hidden.
+2. **Server** (服务器): a menu of the saved servers, shown as name and address. Below it **Add server…** (添加服务器), and for the chosen server **Edit** and **Remove**. The add and edit sheet has Name (optional) and Address (`host`, `host:port`, `[IPv6]:port`, placeholder `relay.example.com:11470`). Validation is live: an empty host, spaces, a scheme or a path, or a port outside 1 to 65535 shows a red line under the field and disables Save. A missing port means 11470, as in the client. A pasted `ldnr://` prefix is removed.
+3. **Test connection** (测试连接): connects to the chosen server with a 5 second limit, sends HELLO, reads HELLO_ACK, sends one PING and times the PONG. The result line is one of
+   - green: "Connected · 38 ms · protocol 1.2 · <server name>"
+   - "Not reachable: <reason>" (no route, refused, timed out, name not found)
+   - "This is not an LDN relay server"
+   - "The server speaks an older protocol" (ERROR VERSION_UNSUPPORTED, a HELLO_ACK minor below 2, or a HELLO_ACK without the capabilities the game needs, 0x1e of the 0x1f it asks for)
+   The test never runs by itself. It uses no game data and sends no password. A server name that needs a DNS lookup can take longer than the 5 second socket timeout, because the lookup itself has no time limit. A server fixed by an env file is tested exactly as the host gets it, so a value the host would not read as written (an `ldnr://` prefix, a trailing `/`) is not tested or shared. On iPhone the first connection to a relay on the local network asks for the Local Network permission (`NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription` in `dm/ios/project.yml`).
+4. **Player name** (玩家名称): a text field. Empty uses the device name (on iPhone the system name, often just "iPhone", so the placeholder suggests setting one). The counter shows the bytes left of the native 32-byte field. Longer names are cut at a character boundary, never inside a character.
+5. **Room password** (房间密码), optional: a secure field. A room password keeps strangers on the same server out of your room. It is cut at a character boundary to the host's 64-byte passphrase field, and the counter shows the bytes left of 64. When it is set, a warning appears: "The relay connection is not encrypted. The password and the game traffic can be read on the network. Use a password only on a trusted network or a VPN, and never one you use elsewhere. The server must allow passwords without TLS." With it the toggle **Send the password unencrypted** (允许明文发送密码). Without that consent online play does not start: Play writes no `DM_LDNR*` variable, the log says that it waits for the password consent, and the section shows "Online play will not start until you allow the password" in red. Clearing the password clears the consent. A `DM_LDNR_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_PASSWORD` set by an env file counts as consent, and its value decides in place of the toggle. A password fixed by an env file is left to the file, so it never waits for the app's consent, and the toggle never allows it. Only the file's own `DM_LDNR_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_PASSWORD=1` does.
+6. **Invite a friend** (邀请好友): **Copy invite** copies a short text with the server and, when one is set, the room password, and a link of the form `nx2apple-ldn://join?server=<address>&password=<password>&name=<server name>`. The invite carries the effective server and password, so a server or password fixed by an env file is the one friends get. **Paste invite** reads the clipboard, adds the server to the list when it is new, chooses it, fills in the password and asks again for the plaintext consent. When `DM_LDNR_SERVER` is fixed, a pasted invite for another server is refused and the section says so. Anything that is not an invite shows "The clipboard has no invite".
+7. A footer under the section: "Status during play is in the game's log (lines starting with ldnr)."
+
+When online play is on but no valid server is chosen, Play still starts the game offline and the section says "Choose a server to play online".
+
+## Flows
+
+**Host a game with friends.** Start the relay (`docker compose up -d` in ldn-relay-server, TCP port 11470 open to the internet). Open the game, turn on Online play, Add server with the public address, Test connection, optionally set a room password and allow it, then Copy invite and send it to friends. Play, then create a room in the game's local wireless menu.
+
+**Join a friend.** Copy the invite from the chat. Open the game, turn on Online play, Paste invite, Test connection, Play, then search for rooms in the game's local wireless menu.
+
+**Switch servers.** Pick another server in the Server menu (or paste a new invite). The next Play uses it. Changing the server needs the game to restart, because the host reads the settings once when the relay starts.
+
+**iPhone and Mac together.** Both use the same server and room password. The same invite text works on both.
+
+## Not in this version
+
+- An in-game status indicator. The host has no overlay for this yet, so the status stays in the log. A later version can show it in the HUD.
+- QR codes and a camera scanner. The invite text travels through any chat app.
+- A per-game server on Mac. The section is designed so a per-game override can be added later without moving the keys.
+- An encrypted transport. See the transport note in LDN-RELAY.md.
+
+## Tests
+
+`dm/ios/tests/test_online_play.py` compiles `OnlinePlay.swift` with `dm/ios/tests/online_play_check.swift` and runs it: address validation, name truncation, invite round trip and parsing, the environment written for every combination (off, on, no server, password with and without consent, values set by env.txt), persistence through a private `UserDefaults` suite, the HELLO and PING frames byte for byte, and the connection test against a fake relay on loopback (a good HELLO_ACK, VERSION_UNSUPPORTED, garbage, silence until the timeout, a closed port). It runs on Mac with Xcode, and on Linux when a Swift toolchain is installed. `test_launcher_storage.py` builds the Mac launcher with the new files, so the view is compiled too.
